<section class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
  <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6 transition-colors hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-primary">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.05em] text-primary">Declarative</p>
    <h2 class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold">No imperative code</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
      No refs. No manually attaching event listeners. Just HTML attributes and
      SpruceX's reactivity doing the heavy lifting.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6 transition-colors hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-primary">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.05em] text-primary">Lightweight</p>
    <h2 class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold">Ship less, do more</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
      Tree-shakeable modules mean you import exactly what you use. A basic bar chart adds
      roughly 12kb to your bundle. That's it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6 transition-colors hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-primary">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.05em] text-primary">Inclusive</p>
    <h2 class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold">Accessibility isn't optional</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
      Every chart includes ARIA labels, keyboard navigation, and high-contrast themes. Built
      to WCAG AA from day one—no retrofitting required.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6 transition-colors hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-primary">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.05em] text-primary">Quality</p>
    <h2 class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold">Print-quality vectors</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
      SVG means crisp lines at any zoom. Export to SVG for reports, PNG for sharing, or
      CSV so your users can work with the raw data.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-12 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
  <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6">
    <div class="grid gap-3">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.05em] text-primary">The Good</p>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Use SpruceX Charts if…</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>You're building a SpruceX app and want charts without writing JS.</li>
      <li>You need clean, professional visualizations without fighting an API.</li>
      <li>Bundle size matters to you.</li>
      <li>Accessibility is a requirement, not an afterthought.</li>
      <li>You want zoom, pan, tooltips, and export out of the box.</li>
      <li>You're building dashboards, reports, or admin interfaces.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6">
    <div class="grid gap-3">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.05em] text-destructive">The Bad</p>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Look elsewhere if…</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>You need to render 10,000+ points in real-time (try Canvas/WebGL).</li>
      <li>You're not using SpruceX (the declarative syntax depends on it).</li>
      <li>You need chart types we don't support (chord, force-directed, etc.).</li>
      <li>You need to support IE11 or browsers without SVG support.</li>
      <li>You want server-rendered chart images (use a dedicated tool for that).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

